<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/algorithms-searching-and-sorting">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Searching and sorting are the hello-world of algorithms — and the clearest demonstration that
    <em>how</em> you compute matters more than how fast your chip is. Same list, same machine:
    one sorting method grinds through n² comparisons, another through n log n. At a billion
    items, that is the difference between days and under a minute.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="naive vs divide-and-conquer — the curves part company early and never look back" widget="plotter" config={sortCfg} />

  <h2>The classic algorithms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Linear search:</strong> check every item — O(n). Fine for ten items; absurd for a billion.</li>
    <li><strong>Binary search:</strong> on a <em>sorted</em> list, halve the remaining candidates each step — O(log n). A billion items resolve in 30 comparisons. The phone book's trick.</li>
    <li><strong>Bubble/selection sort:</strong> compare neighbours, swap, repeat — O(n²). Easy to write, painful to run; the teaching example that everyone replaces.</li>
    <li><strong>Merge sort:</strong> split in half, sort each, merge — O(n log n), guaranteed. The recursive pattern (divide, conquer, combine) behind much of computer science.</li>
    <li><strong>Quick sort:</strong> partition around a pivot, sort the sides — O(n log n) on average, and cache-friendly enough to be the real-world default (your language's sort() is a descendant).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why correctness is part of the deal</h2>
  <p>
    An algorithm is a proof object: each one carries an argument that it terminates and produces
    the right answer. Binary search's famous history of subtly wrong implementations (off-by-one
    boundaries in published code for decades) shows why — the idea is easy, the boundary details
    are where bugs live.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming Vol. 3 (Sorting and Searching); Cormen et al., Introduction to Algorithms.</p>
</TopicPage>
